\documentclass{article}
\title{Direct Recycling of Lithium-Ion Battery Cathodes}
\author{Anon}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
The rapid growth of electric vehicles produces large volumes of spent batteries. Direct recycling is attractive because it preserves the cathode crystal structure. Unlike pyrometallurgy, direct recycling keeps the crystal structure intact and avoids breaking the material down to metals. This structure preservation is the key advantage: the cathode particles retain their layered framework, so relithiation restores capacity with little energy input. Hydrometallurgy dissolves the material and loses the structure entirely. Because the structure survives, direct recycling consumes far less energy than producing new cathode material from ore. We present a relithiation process that repairs degraded cathodes at low temperature.
\section{Process}
Spent cathodes are treated in a lithium-rich solution at moderate temperature.
\end{document}
